1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,695
Why would we normalize every coordinate?

2
00:00:08,695 --> 00:00:15,371
Well, gradient descent is slow along weak directions, which helps it resist noise, but it's also just slow.

3
00:00:15,371 --> 00:00:22,421
The ideal fix would divide each direction by its singular value, but an SVD of a huge model is far too expensive.

4
00:00:22,421 --> 00:00:30,100
So we do the next best thing, and make the steps about the same size in every ordinary coordinate.

5
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,195
What's the crudest way to do that?

6
00:00:32,195 --> 00:00:37,189
Divide each gradient coordinate by its own size, and all that's left is the sign.

7
00:00:37,189 --> 00:00:42,984
Gradients of one hundred, one hundredth and minus three all become steps of plus or minus eta,

8
00:00:42,984 --> 00:00:46,733
so the scale just stops mattering.

9
00:00:46,733 --> 00:00:48,319
Let's try it in the ravine.

10
00:00:48,319 --> 00:00:52,724
It races to the valley floor, because every coordinate moves at full speed.

11
00:00:52,724 --> 00:00:56,776
But then it's stuck, because a step of fixed size keeps overshooting,

12
00:00:56,776 --> 00:01:01,766
so it bounces between two points near the minimum forever.

13
00:01:01,766 --> 00:01:03,730
Adam smooths both of those pieces.

14
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:09,795
It keeps a running average of the gradient, called the first moment, and a running average of its square,

15
00:01:09,795 --> 00:01:10,835
the second moment.

16
00:01:10,835 --> 00:01:18,200
The catch is that both start at zero, so with beta at zero point nine the first average is only a tenth of the gradient.

17
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:19,862
Bias correction fixes that.

18
00:01:19,862 --> 00:01:28,566
We divide each average by one minus its beta raised to the step count, and that scales the early averages back up.

19
00:01:28,566 --> 00:01:35,173
The update then divides the corrected first moment by the square root of the second, one coordinate at a time.

20
00:01:35,173 --> 00:01:40,158
If we skip the corrections, the very first step comes out over three times too big,

21
00:01:40,158 --> 00:01:44,166
and with them the ratio is exactly one.

22
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:47,808
Now back to the same ravine, with the same step size.

23
00:01:47,808 --> 00:01:50,901
The thin line is SignSGD, stuck in its cycle.

24
00:01:50,901 --> 00:01:56,133
Adam's averages smooth the path, so it settles down instead of cycling.

25
00:01:56,133 --> 00:02:02,196
Why throwing away the size of the gradient helps isn't fully understood, and the original proof had gaps.

26
00:02:02,196 --> 00:02:05,800
Adam works well in practice anyway.

27
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:07,104
Now for weight decay.

28
00:02:07,104 --> 00:02:14,434
In plain gradient descent, a ridge penalty is the same as shrinking the weights by the same fixed factor at each step.

29
00:02:14,434 --> 00:02:21,000
Put that penalty inside Adam, though, and it gets divided by the second moment like everything else.

30
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,911
Take two weights that both equal one, with typical gradient sizes of ten and zero point zero five.

31
00:02:26,911 --> 00:02:33,800
The penalty ends up shrinking the second weight two hundred times harder than the first.

32
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:40,331
AdamW takes the shrink out and does it as a separate step, so the moments only ever see the data.

33
00:02:40,331 --> 00:02:42,284
Now both weights decay alike.

34
00:02:42,284 --> 00:02:48,681
Watch the convention, though, because lambda wd sits inside the factor one minus eta lambda wd,

35
00:02:48,681 --> 00:02:52,366
and it's not the ridge lambda.

36
00:02:52,366 --> 00:02:54,058
So which optimizer is best?

37
00:02:54,058 --> 00:03:00,702
We can compare them on three axes, namely training speed, where you end up, and how much memory they need.

38
00:03:00,702 --> 00:03:04,713
On memory, momentum stores one extra vector and Adam stores two,

39
00:03:04,713 --> 00:03:08,766
and the gradients and activations cost more on top of that.

40
00:03:08,766 --> 00:03:11,086
And no optimizer wins on every axis.

41
00:03:11,086 --> 00:03:19,733
They all chase a low training loss, but what we really want is generalization, so measure on your own task.

